    <body>
      <sec id="article-167455.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Paratesticular tumors represent a distinctive subset of neoplasms arising from structures adjacent to, but not within, the testes, including neoplasms of the epididymis, spermatic cord, tunica vaginalis, rete testis, mesenchymal elements, and M&#x000fc;llerian remnants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Although rare, they constitute a clinically significant and heterogeneous group of benign and malignant tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinically, their importance stems primarily from their similarity to benign and malignant testicular neoplasms, in which a misdiagnosis can lead to serious health consequences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The vast majority of paratesticular masses (about 70%-75%) are benign, most commonly adenomatoid tumors, lipomas, fibrous pseudotumors, and leiomyomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Of these, the most common are lipomas (40%-45%) and adenomatoid tumors (34%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Fewer than one-third of paratesticular tumors are malignant. Of these, approximately 90% are sarcomas&#x02014;most commonly rhabdomyosarcomas in children, and liposarcomas or leiomyosarcomas in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;In a recent systematic review, 40% of malignant paratesticular tumors were leiomyosarcomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;This systematic review also indicated that malignant paratesticular neoplasms may constitute a much higher percentage of scrotal cancers than previously thought, making proper diagnosis even more critical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Clinically, paratesticular tumors often present as a painless scrotal mass, although patients may also report discomfort, swelling, or, less commonly, acute pain. Owing to their diverse tissues of origin and wide histologic spectrum, these tumors pose a significant diagnostic challenge.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Management strategies must therefore be individualized based on presentation, clinical history, histologic subtype, stage, and patient characteristics. This review provides insights into the clinical spectrum, diagnostic considerations, therapeutic approaches, and prognostic implications associated with paratesticular tumors.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s2" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The precise etiology of most paratesticular tumors remains unknown; however, several risk factors have been proposed, including genetic susceptibility, a history of testicular malignancy, exposure to environmental or occupational toxins, cryptorchidism, testicular microlithiasis, and prior inguinal hernia repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s3" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The incidence of paratesticular tumors is rare, making it difficult to determine the exact number of cases. These tumors most commonly affect men aged 20 to 60. In the pediatric population, paratesticular rhabdomyosarcoma accounts for approximately 7% of all rhabdomyosarcomas and exhibits a bimodal age distribution with peaks at 5 and 16 years (median age 7 years). Embryonal histology accounts for nearly 90% of pediatric cases and is frequently associated with early regional lymph node involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Tumors of the epididymis are uncommon and predominantly benign. Adenomatoid tumors are the most frequent, typically occurring in the third to fourth decades, arising from any segment of the epididymis but most often from the tail.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r12">[12]</xref> Leiomyomas represent the second most common subtype and may be painful or associated with hydroceles. Epididymal cystadenomas, which are bilateral in approximately 30% of cases and strongly associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease, may histologically mimic metastatic renal cell carcinoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Spermatic cord tumors are likewise typically benign, with lipomas being the most common.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Among malignant lesions, rhabdomyosarcoma predominates, followed by leiomyosarcoma, fibrosarcoma, and liposarcoma.</p>
        <p>Understanding these epidemiological trends is crucial in characterizing the demographic patterns and clinical characteristics of paratesticular tumors. This knowledge can influence clinical practice, facilitate diagnosis, improve clinical decision-making, and optimize patient management strategies.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s4" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histopathologically, paratesticular tumors encompass epithelial, mesenchymal, and mesothelial neoplasms. Epithelial tumors include serous, mucinous, clear cell, Brenner, and endometrioid subtypes, with serous histology being the most frequent. Mesenchymal tumors include lipomas, leiomyomas, and sarcomas, whereas mesothelial tumors include adenomatoid tumors and malignant mesothelioma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s5" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Obtaining a detailed history and performing a thorough physical examination are crucial in evaluating a patient with paratesticular tumors. The history should include a detailed account of the patient's symptoms, such as pain, swelling, or scrotal discoloration, as well as the neoplasm's growth characteristics. Any history of trauma or radiation should be documented.</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;physical examination of paratesticular tumors may reveal a painless scrotal mass, which is the most common presenting symptom.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Physical examination should include inspection and palpation of the scrotum to assess for abnormalities, such as masses, tenderness, or changes in the size or shape of the testicles. Inguinal lymph nodes should be examined. The mass may arise from the epididymis, testicular tunics, or rete testis, and its characteristics vary depending on the tumor's histological subtype.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s6" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The evaluation of paratesticular tumors typically begins with imaging studies, such as ultrasound or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), to assess the tumor's size and location.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r17">[17]</xref> The diagnostic workup may also include laboratory studies.</p>
        <p>Laboratory evaluation for paratesticular tumors typically involves assessing&#x000a0;tumor&#x000a0;markers such as alpha-fetoprotein, carbohydrate antigen (CA-125), and beta-human chorionic gonadotropin to rule out germ cell tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;CA-125 serves as both an immunohistochemical and serum marker in the evaluation of paratesticular serous papillary carcinoma, a M&#x000fc;llerian-type tumor of the male.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;However, these markers are not specific to paratesticular tumors and are more commonly associated with testicular germ cell neoplasms.</p>
        <p>Due to its ease of access, lack of radiation exposure, and ability to determine the location and texture of scrotal masses, ultrasonography is typically the first diagnostic method used in the evaluation process. Ultrasound can help determine the extent of the tumor and whether it has spread or invaded nearby structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Ultrasonographic findings suggestive of malignancy include poorly defined, disorganized solid masses with adhesions, evidence of local invasion, heterogeneity, necrotic areas, and hypervascularity.</p>
        <p>Distinguishing&#x000a0;between&#x000a0;benign and malignant lesions solely on ultrasonographic features can be difficult because of overlapping characteristics. In equivocal cases or when ultrasound imaging is inconsistent with clinical findings, MRI is strongly recommended, as it has over 95% accuracy in distinguishing benign from malignant paratesticular neoplasms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Features suggestive of a benign paratesticular neoplasm include well-defined margins, no unusual&#x000a0;adhesions or signs of local invasion, relatively minimal vascularity, minimal bleeding, easy enucleation, lack of invasive features, and no evidence of lymphadenopathy.</p>
        <p>Some of the more significant paratesticular neoplasms are described below.</p>
        <p>Adenomatoid tumors primarily arise from the epididymis, typically originating from the tail portion. However, they may rarely originate from the tunica vaginalis or tunica albuginea, with a typical age at presentation of between 30 and 40 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;These tumors are hamartomatous neoplasms derived from mesothelial tissue, originally from coelomic epithelial cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Adenomatoid paratesticular tumors are generally small (&#x0003c;2 cm), painless, asymptomatic, slow-growing, benign, and solitary solid neoplasms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;If they become torsed and ischemic, they may cause acute scrotal pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Paratesticular adenomatoid tumors are generally unilateral, typically on the left side, and are associated with epidermoid cysts and germinal tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Adenomatoid tumors that originate from the tunica albuginea may simulate testicular neoplasms by an intratesticular growth pattern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;About 14% originate from the testicular tunica.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Ultrasound demonstrates a homogeneously hyperechoic, round or ovoid paratesticular neoplasm with well-defined borders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;These lesions may appear isoechoic relative to the adjacent epididymis and are mostly avascular, with only limited internal blood flow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r40">[40]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Color Doppler ultrasound may show some increased vascularity, but only in larger tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Contrast-enhanced ultrasonography may demonstrate increased vascularity relative to the adjacent testis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Currently, ultrasound alone, even with contrast enhancement, cannot reliably distinguish benign adenomatoid tumors from other paratesticular pathologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>On MRI, typical findings include a single localized neoplasm in the lower pole of the testicle. The neoplasm&#x000a0;frequently exhibits a lens-shaped appearance, but may also appear round, oval, or even cystic with low signal intensity on T2-weighted imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;When contrast is given, increased vascularity is variable; however, the tumor characteristically shows rim enhancement, though the entire tumor may be hypervascular.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Simple surgical excision of these benign lesions is typically curative, and orchiectomy is rarely required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Fibrous pseudotumors&#x000a0;typically present as painless, firm nodules, frequently developing from the testicular tunics, mostly the tunica vaginalis (75%), but may also involve the tunica albuginea, epididymis, and spermatic cord.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;These tumors occur most often in young and middle-aged men, with a peak incidence in the third decade of life, and represent a benign, reactive overgrowth of fibrous tissue resulting from chronic inflammation, infection, foreign-body reaction, surgery, or trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;About 50% demonstrate a correlation with a hydrocele, and 30% have a history of epididymoorchitis or trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Paratesticular fibrous pseudotumors also appear to be associated with various&#x000a0;IgG4-related diseases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>A suggestive history;&#x000a0;ultrasound imaging showing multiple, well-defined, homogeneously hypoechoic lesions measuring 0.5 to 8 cm;&#x000a0;and characteristic MRI findings demonstrating multiple paratesticular masses with very low signal intensity on T2-weighted imaging and low signal intensity on diffusion-weighted imaging and T1-weighted imaging, in contact with the tunica vaginalis, can&#x000a0;typically&#x000a0;confirm the benign nature of the paratesticular neoplasm, eliminating the need for radical orchiectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;Contrast enhancement is variable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Leiomyomas&#x000a0;are rare, slow-growing, benign, painless, and generally asymptomatic soft-tissue solid neoplasms, most commonly found in men in their 50s and 60s, arising from smooth muscle elements of the epididymis, spermatic cord, or testicular tunics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;These tumors may mimic inguinal hernias in presentation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r54">[54]</xref> On ultrasonography, they typically appear benign, with well-defined solid lesions of variable echogenicity, sometimes showing central necrosis or a characteristic whorled&#x000a0;pattern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;Ultrasound can also confirm their extratesticular anatomical location.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>MRI features of paratesticular leiomyoma include an isointense signal on T1-weighted imaging, low signal intensity on T2-weighted imaging, and low contrast enhancement compared to adjacent structures and organs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r54">[54]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;The lesion may be in contact with the Dartos fascia, and contrast enhancement is variable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref> Treatment typically consists of simple excision with testis-sparing surgery, when feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;Frozen sections can be performed in equivocal cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r58">[58]</xref></p>
        <p>Leiomyosarcomas&#x000a0;are malignant neoplasms that originate from smooth muscle tissue of the spermatic cord, scrotum, or the epididymis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r59">[59]</xref>&#x000a0;Ultrasonographic features overlap with other paratesticular lesions, such as benign leiomyomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;Well-differentiated lesions typically appear as well-demarcated, multilobulated, heterogeneous neoplasms that are relatively hypovascular on ultrasound.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;More aggressive high-grade tumors often demonstrate areas of hypervascularity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>MRI findings are more helpful. Well-differentiated low-grade leiomyosarcomas typically contain 75% fat, with the remaining tissue exhibiting thick (&#x0003e;2 mm) nonfatty septations and small (&#x0003c;2 cm) nonlipomatous regions. In contrast, more aggressive lesions often demonstrate an internal nonfatty focus measuring over 1 cm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r62">[62]</xref>&#x000a0;Other MRI findings include low-to-intermediate signal strength on T1-weighted images and intermediate-to-high signal strength on T2-weighted images.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>Treatment of paratesticular leiomyosarcomas is radical inguinal orchiectomy with resection of all affected tissues and high ligation of the spermatic cord.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;Repeat resection in patients with positive margins may help improve their prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r63">[63]</xref> Adjuvant radiotherapy may be used to reduce the local recurrence rate, especially in high-grade or aggressive neoplasms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r64">[64]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r65">[65]</xref>&#x000a0;Chemotherapy may also be used in selected cases.</p>
        <p>Lipomas&#x000a0;are soft, benign masses that arise primarily in the inguinoscrotal region, most commonly involving the spermatic cord. At least 20% of male patients undergoing hernia repair are found to have spermatic cord lipomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r66">[66]</xref>&#x000a0;These lipomas are often found to be an extension of the preperitoneal fat extending through the deep inguinal ring, but may also be primary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r67">[67]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>On ultrasound, lipomas&#x000a0;appear as homogeneous, hyperechoic, avascular lesions, although they may occasionally be hypoechoic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;This appearance is due to their variable histological composition, which may contain different amounts of fibrous, myxoid, and vascular tissue components.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Solid or enhancing structures suggest possible malignancy, such as liposarcoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r68">[68]</xref></p>
        <p>Paratesticular lipomas on MRI appear as well-demarcated, homogeneous neoplasms with high signal intensity on both T1- and T2-weighted images, similar to adipose tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;Complete loss of signal on fat-suppressed imaging and absence of contrast enhancement help distinguish benign lipomas from malignant liposarcomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Simple surgical excision of a paratesticular lipoma is generally curative, but radical inguinal orchiectomy with high cord ligation is recommended for proven or possible liposarcomas, as clear margins are required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r69">[69]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Liposarcomas&#x000a0;account for about 5% to 7% of all spermatic cord sarcomas and are the most common malignant paratesticular tumors in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r8">[8]</xref> These&#x000a0;tumors typically present as large (&#x0003e;5 cm) firm masses with thick (&#x0003e;2 mm) nonfatty septa and increased internal density on imaging, particularly on MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r71">[71]</xref>&#x000a0;Ultrasonographic imaging of liposarcomas is often nonspecific, making it difficult to distinguish them from lipomas, although they may demonstrate&#x000a0;multilobulated heterogeneity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;Malignancy is suggested by older age, larger tumor size, increased number of thick (&#x0003e;2 mm) nonfatty septa, internal nodules or masses, and decreased fat compared with simple lipomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>Liposarcomas tend to exhibit abnormal fat content, with solid or enhancing components suggesting a high-grade malignancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r68">[68]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Low-grade liposarcomas&#x000a0;are mostly fatty tissue with focal enhancement, suggesting possible malignancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r68">[68]</xref>&#x000a0;On MRI, they tend to show signal dropout on fat-suppressed images, high signal intensity on T1-weighted images, and intermediate signal intensity on T2-weighted images.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;Enhanced, delicate septa and solid structures may be&#x000a0;observed on postcontrast imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;Low-grade liposarcomas&#x000a0;tend to invade locally and extend into adjacent organs, such as the testis and epididymis, and may even be&#x000a0;mistaken for an inguinal hernia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r72">[72]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>High-grade neoplasms tend to be heterogeneous solid masses with relatively little fat and may show areas of necrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r68">[68]</xref>&#x000a0;Typical MRI imaging exhibits&#x000a0;nonspecific, low-to-intermediate signal intensity on T1-weighted images and intermediate-to-high signal intensity on T2-weighted images.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r73">[73]</xref>&#x000a0;These neoplasms behave more aggressively and may metastasize hematogenously to distant sites or via lymphatic spread.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r68">[68]</xref>&#x000a0;No general consensus exists regarding the role of lymph node dissections in the management of these tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r73">[73]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>If the diagnosis remains uncertain, an inguinal approach with possible frozen section evaluation is recommended.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Rhabdomyosarcomas are rare, aggressive malignancies originating from the mesenchymal elements of the epididymis, spermatic cord, or testicular tunics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r74">[74]</xref>&#x000a0;These tumors are&#x000a0;the most common malignant paratesticular tumor, particularly in children and adolescents, accounting for approximately 40% of all malignant paratesticular neoplasms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r74">[74]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r75">[75]</xref> Rhabdomyosarcomas&#x000a0;reach&#x000a0;their peak&#x000a0;incidence (50%) during the first decade of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r76">[76]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r77">[77]</xref></p>
        <p>Although rhabdomyosarcomas&#x000a0;typically present as nontender scrotal lumps or nodules, some of these paratesticular neoplasms may be painful and mimic epididymitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r74">[74]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r78">[78]</xref></p>
        <p>Metastatic spread is via the lymphatics to the iliac and para-aortic nodes, although it may also metastasize hematogenously to the liver and lungs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r74">[74]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r79">[79]</xref>&#x000a0;Spermatic cord rhabdomyosarcomas are characterized by their aggressive nature, with approximately 40% of patients presenting with metastases at diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;Ultrasound and MRI imaging are generally nonspecific, as these tumors often demonstrate isointense density with surrounding tissues and organs.&#x000a0;Positron emission tomography/computed tomography (PET/CT) scans are reserved for cases of childhood rhabdomyosarcomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ultrasound typically shows a well-defined, irregular solid mass separate from the testis, demonstrating low-to-intermediate echogenicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r80">[80]</xref> On color Doppler imaging, the lesion appears hypervascular.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;This lesion&#x000a0;may also appear as a poorly defined mass of mixed echogenicity, which cannot be easily distinguished from other paratesticular pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r80">[80]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>On MRI, Paratesticular rhabdomyosarcomas appear as heterogeneous solid masses with variable signal intensity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r83">[83]</xref> These tumors often demonstrate irregular margins, hemorrhage, areas of local necrosis (ill-defined hypoechoic areas), or cysts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r83">[83]</xref>&#x000a0;Unfortunately,&#x000a0;these imaging features are relatively nonspecific and often overlap with findings in other paratesticular neoplasms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r22">[22]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>FDG-PET/CT (F-fludeoxyglucose PET/CT) scans have not demonstrated satisfactory ability to detect nodal metastases and are therefore not recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r84">[84]</xref>&#x000a0;Paratesticular rhabdomyosarcomas in patients aged 10 or older&#x000a0;require&#x000a0;regional lymph node dissections, including retroperitoneal lymph node dissections, to adequately identify metastases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r84">[84]</xref>&#x000a0;Hemiscrotectomy for a scrotal violation during surgery has also not shown any significant benefit in this lesion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r84">[84]</xref></p>
        <p>Treatment is customized and may include radical inguinal orchiectomy, chemotherapy, and radiation, often in a multimodality approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r84">[84]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r85">[85]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r86">[86]</xref>&#x000a0;Five-year survival rates with proper treatment have been reported as over 80%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r74">[74]</xref></p>
        <p>Other paratesticular carcinomas have been reported, including malignant fibrous histiocytomas, dedifferentiated sarcomas, and rare epithelial cancers such as adenocarcinoma of the rete testis and malignant mesothelioma of the tunica vaginalis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r87">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r88">[88]</xref></p>
        <p>Abdominal and pelvic CT, with or without thoracic imaging,&#x000a0;is commonly preferred for evaluating tumor spread.&#x000a0;CT and MRI are also suitable for assessing lymph node involvement in the local, pelvic, or retroperitoneal regions, which can impact subsequent surgical decisions. MRI provides superior soft-tissue contrast and should be considered when ultrasound findings are unclear regarding tumor dimensions, local invasiveness, or possible tunica albuginea involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r89">[89]</xref>&#x000a0;MRI is also helpful for distinguishing the tumor margins from the testicular parenchyma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r89">[89]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s7" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>In general,&#x000a0;clinical features, sonographic studies, MRI imaging, and laboratory evaluations may be inconclusive or equivocal. A definitive diagnosis frequently requires pathological examination of the lesion after surgical excision. Since the diagnosis may be uncertain preoperatively, the surgical approach is typically inguinal, with a tourniquet applied to the proximal spermatic cord, as for testicular malignancies. Frozen sections may be used to determine if orchiectomy can be safely avoided.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r90">[90]</xref>&#x000a0;With some exceptions, a scrotal skin violation during surgery of malignant paratesticular tumors may require hemiscrotectomy.</p>
        <p>Frozen sections can be useful when used selectively in borderline cases, but an inguinal approach is necessary for initial access.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r90">[90]</xref>&#x000a0;If the lesion proves to be malignant, remains equivocal, or cannot be surgically excised from the testis, orchiectomy&#x000a0;is required.&#x000a0;Frozen sections may be used to determine if orchiectomy can be safely avoided based on benign histology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r90">[90]</xref></p>
        <p>Management typically entails an inguinal or scrotal approach for tumor excision for benign tumors. The scrotal approach is appropriate only for benign lesions with a certain diagnosis. For malignant, suspicious, or equivocal lesions,&#x000a0;it is essential to use an inguinal approach and perform a complete radical inguinal orchiectomy, which includes excising surrounding tissue and conducting a high ligation of the spermatic cord at the external inguinal ring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The role of retroperitoneal lymph node dissection in the management of malignant tumors, especially sarcomas, remains debated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r91">[91]</xref>&#x000a0;Adjuvant therapies, including radiation therapy and chemotherapy, are&#x000a0;considered in select cases. Locoregional radiation has been shown to reduce the risk of sarcoma recurrence. No prospectively validated studies have evaluated chemotherapy in adult sarcomas; however, a doxorubicin-based regimen has been used with some success.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r92">[92]</xref>&#x000a0;In children, the doxorubicin-based regimen has shown significant survival benefits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;Adjuvant radiation has also been studied in patients with paratesticular mesotheliomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r94">[94]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s8" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Paratesticular tumors can mimic other benign and malignant conditions of the scrotum, including ectopic testes, epididymitis, hernias, hydroceles, metastatic lesions, lymphomas, hemangiomas, posttraumatic hematomas, spermatoceles, varicoceles, and testicular tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-167455.r95">[95]</xref>&#x000a0;A thorough differential diagnosis is crucial for guiding appropriate management and avoiding unnecessary interventions or overly aggressive surgeries.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s9" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>Ongoing research for paratesticular tumors focuses on improving diagnostic techniques, elucidating the molecular mechanisms of tumorigenesis,&#x000a0;identifying novel biomarkers for early detection and prognosis, refining treatment strategies, and identifying novel therapeutic targets. Advances in molecular profiling and targeted therapies offer promising avenues for the personalized management of paratesticular neoplasms, improving outcomes and quality of life for affected individuals.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s10" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>Paratesticular tumors&#x000a0;are staged&#x000a0;according to the American Joint Committee on Cancer staging system, which incorporates tumor size, extent of invasion, lymph node involvement, and the presence of distant metastases. Accurate staging is crucial for prognostication and treatment decision-making.</p>
        <p>For malignant paratesticular tumors, imaging plays a role in tumor staging. Chest radiography, abdominal ultrasound, or chest, abdominal, and pelvic CT scans should&#x000a0;be used&#x000a0;to accurately stage the&#x000a0;tumor&#x000a0;and assess for retroperitoneal lymph node involvement and pulmonary metastases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of paratesticular tumors varies depending on the histologic subtype, tumor stage, and response to treatment.&#x000a0;Overall,&#x000a0;the prognosis is extremely favorable for benign tumors, with excellent long-term survival rates following surgical resection.&#x000a0;Malignant tumors, however, may carry a higher risk of recurrence and metastasis, necessitating close surveillance and adjuvant therapy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of paratesticular tumors may include local recurrence following surgical resection, as well as the potential for metastatic spread in cases of malignant tumors.</p>
        <p>Common adverse effects of treatment for paratesticular tumors include surgical complications, such as wound infections, hematomas, bruising, and lymphedema.</p>
        <p>Radiation therapy may cause acute and chronic toxicity, including skin&#x000a0;irritation and&#x000a0;fatigue.&#x000a0;Additionally, the psychological impact of a cancer diagnosis and the potential for impaired fertility following treatment are important considerations for patients.</p>
        <p>Long-term follow-up is essential to monitor disease progression and address any treatment-related sequelae.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s13" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Postoperative care for patients undergoing surgical resection of paratesticular tumors involves pain management, wound care, and monitoring for signs of complications, such as infection or hematoma. Patient education regarding self-examination and follow-up appointments is critical for early detection of recurrences or metastases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s14" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Multidisciplinary consultations with adult and pediatric urologists, oncologists, radiologists, and pathologists are essential&#x000a0;for the comprehensive management of&#x000a0;paratesticular tumors.&#x000a0;Genetic counseling may&#x000a0;be indicated&#x000a0;for patients with a family history of genitourinary malignancies or known genetic syndromes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s15" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Paratesticular tumors are rare but require careful attention to ensure timely diagnosis and effective management. A&#x000a0;comprehensive approach that includes self-examination, symptom awareness, understanding of risk factors, prompt medical evaluation, and consistent follow-up care is necessary to optimize outcomes. Self-examination&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;the scrotum is an essential practice that enables individuals to detect abnormalities early.</p>
        <p>Educating&#x000a0;patients about the proper technique for self-examination, which involves manual palpation of the scrotum and surrounding areas, encourages proactive involvement in their health. By&#x000a0;promoting self-awareness and encouraging routine self-checks, healthcare providers can facilitate early detection of paratesticular tumors, enabling timely medical intervention.</p>
        <p>Factors&#x000a0;such as prior radiation exposure, a history of inguinal hernia repair, or a family history of testicular cancer warrant heightened vigilance and may prompt healthcare providers to recommend more frequent screenings or surveillance. By&#x000a0;raising awareness of these risk factors, healthcare providers empower patients to make informed decisions regarding their health and seek proactive medical evaluation when warranted. Encouraging&#x000a0;patients to prioritize the timely&#x000a0;evaluation&#x000a0;of any concerning symptoms or abnormalities is crucial&#x000a0;for early detection, diagnosis, and treatment of paratesticular tumors.</p>
        <p>Prompt&#x000a0;medical assessment enables an expedited diagnostic workup, including imaging studies and biopsy, if indicated, facilitating the timely initiation of appropriate treatment strategies. Patients&#x000a0;diagnosed with these malignancies should be educated&#x000a0;about the necessity of regular follow-up appointments with their healthcare provider to monitor for recurrence or metastasis. Follow-up&#x000a0;care may entail physical examinations, imaging studies, and laboratory tests to assess treatment response and detect any signs of disease progression.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>To ensure the efficient management of paratesticular tumors, a collective effort from a diverse group of healthcare professionals is essential. Urologists, oncologists, radiologists, pathologists, and allied healthcare providers need core competencies in diagnosis, assessment, and treatment modalities to navigate the complexities of these tumors.</p>
        <p>Radiological expertise plays a crucial role in accurately interpreting imaging studies, enabling precise localization and&#x000a0;characterization of&#x000a0;paratesticular masses. Depending on the tumor's characteristics and the patient's clinical history and medical comorbidities, consultation with surgical specialties, such as urology or general surgery, may be necessary to ensure optimal therapeutic planning, treatment, and outcomes.</p>
        <p>Ethical considerations are vital in the treatment, requiring a patient-centered approach that upholds principles of autonomy and beneficence. Interprofessional collaboration and communication within the healthcare team are pivotal, with clearly defined roles and responsibilities that ensure seamless&#x000a0;coordination of care. Open communication channels foster a collaborative environment where expertise is shared, concerns&#x000a0;are addressed, and decisions&#x000a0;are made&#x000a0;collectively, improving the quality of care delivered.</p>
        <p>From diagnosis to post-treatment surveillance, well-coordinated care minimizes inefficiencies, enhances patient safety, and optimizes clinical outcomes. Prioritizing collaboration, communication, and coordination enables healthcare teams to provide comprehensive, patient-centric care that addresses the unique needs and challenges associated with paratesticular tumors, ultimately improving the overall experience and outcomes for affected individuals.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-167455.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=167455&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=167455">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/articlelibrary/commentarticle/167455/?utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=comments&#x00026;utm_content=167455">Comment on this article.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
